/**
\defgroup distrib_independent_product Independent product
\brief Univariate laws as multivariate ones, products of independent blocks, and their penalty descriptors.

Include `<laplace/distrib/univariate_as_multivariate.hpp>` for \ref laplace::distrib::univariate_as_multivariate "univariate_as_multivariate\<D\>", `<laplace/distrib/independent_product.hpp>` for \ref laplace::distrib::independent_product_distrib "independent_product_distrib\<M...\>", and `<laplace/penalty/independent_product_penalty.hpp>` for their penalty descriptors. The distribution classes and their helpers live in namespace `laplace::distrib`, and the penalty descriptors live in namespace `laplace::penalty`. All public member functions on this page are marked `LAPLACE_HD` and can be called on the host and inside Kokkos kernels on the device. In tests over three products on the host and inside a Kokkos kernel, log-densities match component sums within \f$10^{-14}\f$, derivative blocks agree with central differences within \f$10^{-6}\f$ (with kernel and host errors within \f$10^{-9}\f$), and a two-dimensional normalization integral agrees with \f$1\f$ within \f$10^{-9}\f$.

## Univariate adapter

The adapter \ref laplace::distrib::univariate_as_multivariate "univariate_as_multivariate\<D\>" presents any univariate distribution `D`, continuous or discrete, with or without atoms, as a \ref laplace::distrib::multivariate_distrib "multivariate_distrib" (see \ref distrib_multivariate "Multivariate") of dimension \f$d = 1\f$ and normalization `distrib_normalization::proper`. Parameter \f$0\f$ of `D` becomes the dense location parameter \f$\mu\f$ (`distrib_mv_param_kind::vector`) of size \f$1\f$, so `param_size(0)` is \f$1\f$ and `param_offset(0)` is \f$0\f$. Parameters \f$1, \dots, P - 1\f$ of `D` (where \f$P =\f$ `D::n_params`) become `distrib_mv_param_kind::scalar` parameters whose coordinates form \f$\theta\f$ of length \f$P - 1\f$ in `D`'s order, with `theta_size()` equal to \f$P - 1\f$, `param_size(j)` equal to \f$1\f$, and `param_offset(j)` equal to \f$j - 1\f$ for \f$j \ge 1\f$. Each descriptor in `params` keeps `D`'s `name`, `lower`, `upper`, and `regularity`.

The adapter keeps a prototype of `D`, passed to its constructor, which holds what is not a parameter: truncation bounds, a map, or atom points. At each call it copies the prototype and writes \f$(x_0, \theta)\f$ over the first \f$P\f$ doubles of the copy, where \f$x_0\f$ is the location and \f$\theta\f$ holds the other parameters. So `D` must be trivially copyable, and its \f$P\f$ parameters must be its first \f$P\f$ doubles in `params` order. A static assertion checks this with the message "univariate_as_multivariate: D must be trivially copyable and hold its parameters as its first doubles". An aggregate of its parameters satisfies it, and so does a wrapper that holds its parent first and then its own parameters.

The kind of the adapter is that of `D`. A discrete `D` gives `logpmf` and `logpmf_dk`, with no derivatives in \f$y\f$. A continuous `D` gives `logpdf`, `logpdf_dk` and `logpdf_dy_dk`.

The atoms of `D` are the coordinate atoms of coordinate \f$0\f$: `n_coord_atoms(0)` returns their number and `coord_atom_point(0, a)` the position of atom \f$a\f$, when `D` declares atoms (see \ref distrib_atoms "the atoms of a distribution"). At an atom the value is the log mass.

The bounds of \f$\mu\f$ are those of `D`'s parameter 0. A mean with a positive domain, as in gamma1, poisson or negbin2, stays positive, because the descriptor carries the domain and no link is applied to \f$\mu\f$.

Its state type is \ref laplace::distrib::univariate_as_multivariate_state "univariate_as_multivariate_state", which stores only `const double* theta`. Both `state_size()` and `scratch_size()` return \f$0\f$, and `theta_state(theta, w)` returns `{theta}` without writing to `w`; the caller must keep `theta` alive while the state is in use. Each call to `logpdf()` or a derivative function reconstructs `D` from `(x[0], st.theta)` as an aggregate of \f$P\f$ doubles.

A sorted index tuple `idx` of length \f$K\f$ over \f$\theta\f$ corresponds to the tuple `idx + 1` over `D`'s parameters. For \f$J + K + A \le 6\f$ (with omitted \f$A\f$ defaulting to \f$0\f$), `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K, A>(st, y, x, idx, out, w)` evaluates `D::logpdf_dy_dk<J, K + A>(y[0])` and writes one `double` into `out[0]`: entry \f$0\f$ when \f$K = A = 0\f$, and otherwise the component \f$(0^A, \mathrm{idx} + 1)\f$ (\f$A\f$ zeros followed by `idx[i] + 1` for \f$0 \le i < K\f$) located by `sym_tensor_position<n_params, K + A>`. With \f$J = 0\f$ the call reads `D::logpdf_dk`, so a law without derivatives in \f$y\f$ still gives its parameter derivatives. The call always returns `true`. For a continuous `D`, `logpdf_dk<K, A>` forwards to `logpdf_dy_dk<0, K, A>`. For a discrete `D`, `logpmf_dk<K, A>` writes the same component of `D::logpmf_dk<K + A>(y[0])`.

## Independent product

The distribution \ref laplace::distrib::independent_product_distrib "independent_product_distrib\<M_1, ..., M_n\>" (\f$n \ge 1\f$) forms the product of \f$n\f$ independent multivariate components, each repeated over its copies:
\f[
\log p(y \mid \mu, \theta) = \sum_k \sum_c \log p_{M_k}(y_{kc} \mid \mu_{kc}, \theta_{k, h_k(c)}).
\f]
Here \f$k = 0, \dots, n - 1\f$ indexes the component distributions \f$M_k\f$, each of dimension \f$d_k\f$, and \f$c = 0, \dots, G_k - 1\f$ indexes the \f$G_k\f$ copies of component \f$k\f$. Component \f$k\f$ has \f$H_k\f$ parameter sets indexed by \f$h = 0, \dots, H_k - 1\f$, and the map \f$h_k(c) \in \{0, \dots, H_k - 1\}\f$ selects the parameter set used by copy \f$c\f$.

Each component is passed as an \ref laplace::distrib::independent_product_component "independent_product_component\<M_k\>" holding `distribution` (`M_k`), `copies` (\f$G_k\f$), `n_sets` (\f$H_k\f$), and `set_of_copy`. Its `set(c)` member evaluates \f$h_k(c)\f$: it returns `set_of_copy[c]` when `set_of_copy` is non-null (the pointer is non-owning and not copied), \f$0\f$ when `set_of_copy` is `nullptr` and `n_sets == 1` (all copies share one parameter set), and \f$c\f$ when `set_of_copy` is `nullptr` and `n_sets == copies` (one set per copy).

The observation vector \f$y\f$ and location vector \f$\mu\f$ are concatenated copy by copy within each component, component by component, so `dim()` returns \f$\sum_k G_k d_k\f$. Each copy forms one block: `n_blocks()` returns \f$\sum_k G_k\f$, and `block_start(slot, g)` (for `y_slot` or parameter \f$0\f$, with \f$0 \le g \le\f$ `n_blocks()`) returns the starting coordinate of block \f$g\f$, or `dim()` when \f$g =\f$ `n_blocks()`.

At compile time, `independent_product_distrib` checks that there is at least one component, that each `M_k` has exactly one dense parameter (its location, at index \f$0\f$), and that `M_k::state_type` is trivially copyable. Its `normalization` is `distrib_normalization::improper` if any component is improper, `distrib_normalization::degenerate` if none is improper and at least one is degenerate, and `distrib_normalization::proper` otherwise.

The components are all continuous or all discrete, and a static assertion rejects a mix. A continuous product has `logpdf`. A discrete product has `logpmf`, `logpmf_dk_blocks` and `logpmf_dk`, in the layout of the continuous routes with \f$J = 0\f$.

Components may have atoms on their coordinates. The reference measure of a continuous product, over the coordinates \f$y_i\f$ of \f$y\f$, is the product of Lebesgue measure and counting measure on the atoms of each coordinate. So `logpdf` is the log density with respect to that measure: a coordinate that sits on an atom of its copy contributes the log mass of that atom, and any other coordinate contributes its log density. The mass of the product therefore lies on the hyperplanes \f$y_i = c\f$, where \f$c\f$ is an atom of the copy that owns coordinate \f$i\f$. `n_coord_atoms(i)` and `coord_atom_point(i, a)` report these atoms, read from the copy that owns coordinate \f$i\f$ at its own coordinate. They exist when a component declares atoms. Derivatives in a coordinate of \f$y\f$ that sits on one of its atoms have no meaning.

## Parameters and workspace

The indexed array \f$\theta\f$ has total length `theta_size()` equal to \f$\sum_k H_k q_k\f$, where \f$q_k =\f$ `M_k::theta_size()`, and is ordered param-major. For each component \f$k\f$ and each indexed parameter \f$j = 1, \dots, P_k - 1\f$ of `M_k` (where \f$P_k =\f$ `M_k::n_params`), the \f$H_k\f$ copies of parameter \f$j\f$ sit side by side. For instance, a Student t component with two parameter sets orders \f$\theta\f$ as \f$(\sigma_0, \sigma_1, \nu_0, \nu_1)\f$.

The parameter descriptor table `params` has `n_params` equal to \f$1 + \sum_k (P_k - 1)\f$. Entry \f$0\f$ is `"mu"` (`distrib_mv_param_kind::vector`, bounds \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$, and regularity equal to the least `M_k::params[0].regularity` over all components). Each subsequent entry belongs to one pair of component \f$k\f$ and indexed parameter \f$j\f$, preserving `M_k`'s `name`, `kind`, `lower`, `upper`, and `regularity`. Consequently, `param_size(0)` equals `dim()`, and for an indexed parameter of component \f$k\f$ it equals \f$H_k\f$ times `M_k`'s parameter size; `param_offset(j)` is \f$0\f$ for \f$j = 0\f$ and the sum of preceding indexed `param_size()` values for \f$j \ge 1\f$.

The bounds of each coordinate come from the copy or the set that owns it. `coord_lower(j, i)` and `coord_upper(j, i)` return the bound of coordinate \f$i\f$ of parameter \f$j\f$, taken from the copy that owns coordinate \f$i\f$ of \f$\mu\f$ when \f$j = 0\f$, and from the set that owns it otherwise. Each reads the component's own bound through `mv_coord_lower()` or `mv_coord_upper()`, which fall back to the component's `params` bounds. So the bounds of \f$\mu\f$ can differ from copy to copy, while the entry 0 of `params` is unbounded.

The state type is \ref laplace::distrib::independent_product_state "independent_product_state", holding `const double* regions`. Calling `theta_state(theta, w)` partitions `w[0, state_size())` into one region per component set \f$(k, h)\f$. For each set, it reserves the size of `M_k::state_type` rounded up to whole `double`s, gathers the set's \f$q_k\f$ parameters into `w` via `gather_set_theta()`, and calls `M_k::theta_state()` on that copy using the trailing memory as scratch. `state_size()` returns the total `double` count of all per-set regions, and `scratch_size()` returns the largest `M_k::scratch_size()` across components. The caller must pass `w` of length `state_size() + scratch_size()` doubles and keep `w`, `theta`, and any `set_of_copy` arrays alive while the state is in use. The helper `each(f)` visits the components in order as `f(component, std::integral_constant<int, k>{})`.

## Derivative blocks and dense layout

A sorted index tuple `idx` of length \f$K\f$ over \f$\theta\f$ whose entries belong to two distinct sets (either two components or two sets of the same component) corresponds to zero mixed partial derivatives by independence. Both derivative routes return `false` and leave `out` untouched for such a tuple.

The block route `logpdf_dy_dk_blocks<J, K, A...>(st, y, x, idx, out, w)` writes, copy after copy, the output of `M_k::logpdf_dy_dk<J, K, A...>` for that copy (\f$\binom{d_k + J - 1}{J}\binom{d_k + A - 1}{A}\f$ doubles), or zeros when the copy belongs to a set outside `idx` or `M_k` returns `false`. Order \f$0\f$ (\f$J = A = 0\f$) is the scalar sum over all copies, written to `out[0]`. The buffer `out` must hold \ref laplace::distrib::mv_block_tensor_size "mv_block_tensor_size\<A...\>(*this, J)" doubles, and `w` must hold the components' scratch (`scratch_size()`, plus \f$\binom{d_k + J + A - 1}{J + A}\f$ doubles when `A > 0` for components that expand mixed \f$(y, \mu)\f$ blocks from a higher-order \f$\mu\f$ tensor). The block layout and the free helpers \ref laplace::distrib::mv_n_blocks "mv_n_blocks()", \ref laplace::distrib::mv_block_start "mv_block_start()", \ref laplace::distrib::mv_block_tensor_size "mv_block_tensor_size()", and \ref laplace::distrib::mv_logpdf_dy_dk_blocks "mv_logpdf_dy_dk_blocks()" are documented on \ref distrib_multivariate "Multivariate".

The dense route `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K, A...>(st, y, x, idx, out, w)` delegates to `logpdf_dy_dk_blocks` when \f$J = A = 0\f$. Otherwise, it evaluates the block route into `w` after the components' scratch and scatters each block's tensor into the packed symmetric tensors over `dim()` (order \f$J\f$ over \f$y\f$, Kronecker product row-major with \f$y\f$ first with order \f$A\f$ over \f$\mu\f$), setting every entry coupling two distinct blocks to \f$0\f$. Because the dense output grows as \f$d^{J + A}\f$, this route is intended for tests and small dimensions; its scratch `w` must hold the components' scratch followed by `mv_block_tensor_size<A...>(*this, J)` doubles. The members `logpdf_dk_blocks<K, A...>` and `logpdf_dk<K, A...>` are the corresponding routes with \f$J = 0\f$.

## Penalty reading

Both `univariate_as_multivariate<D>` and `independent_product_distrib<M...>` specialize \ref laplace::penalty::distrib_penalty_traits "distrib_penalty_traits", whose descriptors are defined on \ref penalty_framework "Framework".

For `univariate_as_multivariate<D>`, `beta_regularity` equals `D::y_regularity`, `kinks` is `penalty_kink_geometry::per_coordinate` when `D::y_regularity` is finite and `penalty_kink_geometry::none` when it is `infinitely_smooth`, `structure` is `penalty_structure::separable`, `theta_regularity` is the least `regularity` among `D`'s scalar parameters, and `q_linear_in_theta` is `false`. The `scale_mixture` descriptor and `weak_convexity(theta)` (which returns the weak-convexity constant \f$\rho = \sup(-\mathrm{pen}'')\f$) come from \ref laplace::penalty::univariate_penalty_reading "univariate_penalty_reading\<D\>". Its primary template is undefined so unsupported laws fail to compile, and eight specializations are provided (all with `theta_regularity` equal to `infinitely_smooth`).

| Law | Penalty | Regularity in \f$\beta\f$ | Kinks | Scale mixture | \f$\rho\f$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| \ref distrib_laplace1 "laplace1_distrib" | Lasso (\f$\lvert\beta - \mu\rvert / \sigma\f$) | \f$0\f$ | `per_coordinate` | `exponential_variance` | \f$0\f$ |
| \ref distrib_laplace2 "laplace2_distrib" | Lasso with a rate (\f$\lambda\lvert\beta - \mu\rvert\f$) | \f$0\f$ | `per_coordinate` | `exponential_variance` | \f$0\f$ |
| \ref distrib_enet "enet_distrib" | Elastic net | \f$0\f$ | `per_coordinate` | `none` | \f$0\f$ |
| \ref distrib_student_t1 "student_t1_distrib" | Student (\f$\sigma\f$ scale) | `infinitely_smooth` | `none` | `inverse_gamma_variance` | \f$(\nu + 1)/(8\nu\sigma^2)\f$ |
| \ref distrib_student_t2 "student_t2_distrib" | Student (\f$\sigma\f$ std. dev.) | `infinitely_smooth` | `none` | `inverse_gamma_variance` | \f$(\nu + 1)/(8(\nu - 2)\sigma^2)\f$ |
| \ref distrib_pseudohuber "pseudohuber_distrib" | Pseudo-Huber | `infinitely_smooth` | `none` | `none` | \f$0\f$ |
| \ref distrib_gaussian1 "gaussian1_distrib" | Ridge (\f$\sigma\f$ std. dev.) | `infinitely_smooth` | `none` | `none` | \f$0\f$ |
| \ref distrib_gaussian2 "gaussian2_distrib" | Ridge (\f$v\f$ variance) | `infinitely_smooth` | `none` | `none` | \f$0\f$ |

### Weak convexity of the Student penalties

For `student_t1_distrib`, writing \f$u = (\beta - \mu)^2 / (\nu \sigma^2)\f$, the second derivative of the penalty in \f$\beta\f$ is
\f[
\mathrm{pen}''(\beta) = \frac{\nu + 1}{\nu \sigma^2} \frac{1 - u}{(1 + u)^2}.
\f]
Its least value is attained at \f$u = 3\f$ and equals \f$-(\nu + 1) / (8 \nu \sigma^2)\f$, so \f$\rho = (\nu + 1) / (8 \nu \sigma^2)\f$. For `student_t2_distrib`, the standard deviation \f$\sigma\f$ corresponds to scale \f$s = \sigma \sqrt{(\nu - 2) / \nu}\f$, so substituting \f$s\f$ for \f$\sigma\f$ yields \f$\rho = (\nu + 1) / (8 \nu s^2) = (\nu + 1) / (8 (\nu - 2) \sigma^2)\f$.

### Traits of an independent product

For `independent_product_distrib<M...>`, `distrib_penalty_traits` aggregates the components' traits: `beta_regularity` and `theta_regularity` are each the least over all components; `kinks` is the most general geometry present among the components (`none`, `per_coordinate`, `radial`, `linear_map`); `structure` is `penalty_structure::separable` when every component is separable and `penalty_structure::grouped` otherwise; `q_linear_in_theta` is `true` when it is `true` for every component and `false` otherwise; and `scale_mixture` is that of the component when there is a single component type (`sizeof...(M) == 1`) and `penalty_scale_mixture::none` otherwise.

The member `weak_convexity(m, theta)` returns the largest \f$\rho\f$ over all parameter sets of every component. For each set \f$h\f$, `gather_set_theta()` copies the set's parameters into a fixed buffer of \f$32\f$ doubles; any component with `theta_size() > 32` reports \f$+\infty\f$ (`distrib_infinity`), a safe upper bound. As described on \ref penalty_distrib_penalty "Distribution penalty", \ref laplace::penalty::distrib_penalty "distrib_penalty\<M\>" calls this two-argument `weak_convexity(distribution, theta)` overload whenever the traits provide it.

The proximal operator, when it exists, is forwarded in the same way. `univariate_as_multivariate<D>::prox_y` exists when `D` has a `prox_y` member, and it returns `D::prox_y` at the location \f$x_0\f$. `independent_product_distrib<M...>::prox_y` exists when every component has one. Because the log-density is a sum over copies, the product applies to each copy the `prox_y` of its component, with the parameter set of that copy. The operator itself is defined on the page \ref penalty_prox "Proximal operators".

## Accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for test conventions. Automated tests in `tests/penalty/test_independent_product.cpp` exercise three products alongside single-law checks: a \f$4\f$-copy lasso product (`laplace1_distrib`) with a shared scale and prior location \f$\mu_0\f$; a \f$5\f$-copy Student product (`student_t1_distrib`) in \f$2\f$ parameter sets (`{0, 1, 0, 1, 1}`); and a heterogeneous product combining \f$2\f$ shared `enet_distrib` copies, \f$2\f$ dimension-\f$2\f$ `mvgaussian2_distrib` copies with `unstructured_pdmat<log_cholesky_bijector>` by copy, and \f$1\f$ `pseudohuber_distrib` copy (dimension \f$7\f$, \f$5\f$ blocks, \f$10\f$ indexed parameters). Normalization is checked on a \f$2\f$-dimensional product of `pseudohuber_distrib` and `gaussian2_distrib` with \f$\mu_0\f$.

| Check | Scope | Criterion or tolerance |
|---|---|---|
| Univariate adapter tensors | `univariate_as_multivariate<student_t2_distrib>` `logpdf()` and blocks \f$(0, 1, 0)\f$, \f$(0, 0, 2)\f$, \f$(2, 2, 1)\f$, \f$(1, 2, 0)\f$ | Exact equality with `student_t2_distrib` |
| Twin log-density | Student and heterogeneous products, host and Kokkos kernel | Relative error below \f$10^{-14}\f$ against component sum |
| Derivative finite differences | All \f$13\f$ dense blocks \f$(J, K, A)\f$ with \f$J \le 2\f$, \f$K \le 3\f$, \f$A \le 1\f$, \f$1 \le J + K + A \le 3\f$, host and kernel | Normwise relative error below \f$10^{-6}\f$ (central differences, step \f$10^{-5}\f$ times \f$1\f$ or the coordinate magnitude, whichever is larger) |
| Kernel and host match | Twin and all \f$13\f$ finite-difference errors | Absolute difference below \f$10^{-9}\f$ |
| Dense against block route | Heterogeneous product, blocks \f$(2, K, 0)\f$ and \f$(1, K, 1)\f$ for \f$K = 0, 1, 2\f$ | Exact equality on diagonal blocks, \f$0\f$ off-block |
| Tuples across sets | Heterogeneous product, `{4, 5}` (copies \f$0\f$ and \f$1\f$ of \f$Q\f$) and `{0, 8}` (`enet` and `pseudohuber`) | Returns `false` and leaves `out` untouched |
| Penalty twin and blocks | Lasso (\f$4\f$ copies, with \f$\mu_0\f$) and heterogeneous (zero location) | Lasso `value()` and `<0, 1>` within \f$10^{-14}\f$, `<1, 1>` exact, `<2, 0>` and `weak_convexity()` equal \f$0\f$; heterogeneous `value()` within \f$10^{-13}\f$, `weak_convexity()` equal \f$0\f$ |
| Student \f$\rho\f$ | `student_t1_distrib` per set on \f$[0, 20\sigma)\f$ (step \f$10^{-4}\sigma\f$); `student_t2_distrib` at \f$\sigma = 1.5\f$, \f$\nu = 5\f$ | Relative error below \f$10^{-7}\f$ against \f$-\min\mathrm{pen}''\f$, product equals set maximum; `student_t2_distrib` within \f$10^{-15}\f$ |
| Normalization integral | `pseudohuber_distrib` and `gaussian2_distrib` around \f$\mu_0\f$ | Trapezoid rule on \f$[-30, 30] \times [-8, 8]\f$ (step \f$0.02\f$) within \f$10^{-9}\f$ of \f$1\f$ |

The univariate adapter, the discrete and atom-carrying products and their bounds are tested in `tests/distrib/test_univariate_as_multivariate.cpp`. The laws are a zero-adjusted gamma1 and a zero-adjusted gaussian2, both with an atom at \f$0\f$, a poisson and a negbin2. The tolerances below are those of the test file.

| Check | Scope | Criterion or tolerance |
|---|---|---|
| Adapter against the rebuilt law, gamma1 | Zero-adjusted gamma1 on the atom and off it: value and parameter derivatives to order 3 | Exact equality with the law rebuilt by hand |
| Adapter against the rebuilt law, gaussian2 | Zero-adjusted gaussian2 on the atom and off it: value | Exact equality |
| Derivatives against central differences | Zero-adjusted gamma1 at \f$y = 0, 0.8, 3.1\f$, zero-adjusted gaussian2 at \f$y = 0, -0.4, 1.3\f$, poisson and negbin2 | Relative error below \f$10^{-7}\f$, denominator \f$\max(1, \lvert a \rvert)\f$, for the first derivatives in \f$\theta\f$ and \f$\mu\f$ and the mixed ones; step \f$10^{-6}\f$ times the larger of \f$1\f$ and the coordinate's magnitude |
| Descriptors | Coordinate atoms and bounds of the laws listed above | Exact equality with the expected values |
| Total mass, zero-adjusted gamma1 | Mass of the atom plus the sum of the density times step \f$10^{-3}\f$ on \f$(0, 40)\f$ | Within \f$10^{-9}\f$ of \f$1\f$ |
| Total mass, zero-adjusted gaussian2 | Mass of the atom plus the sum of the density times step \f$10^{-2}\f$ on \f$[-12, 12]\f$ | Within \f$10^{-12}\f$ of \f$1\f$ |
| Total mass, poisson and negbin2 | Sums over \f$y = 0, \dots, 199\f$ | Within \f$10^{-13}\f$ of \f$1\f$ |
| Product twin, continuous | Two zero-adjusted gamma1 copies with their own \f$\theta\f$, one zero-adjusted gaussian2 copy | Within \f$10^{-14}\f$ of the sum of the component log densities |
| Product derivatives | The same product, central differences with step \f$10^{-6}\f$ | In \f$\theta\f$: relative error below \f$10^{-7}\f$, denominator \f$\max(1, \lvert g \rvert)\f$; in \f$\mu\f$: absolute error below \f$10^{-7}\f$ |
| Discrete product | Two poisson copies and one negbin2 copy: log mass and its derivative in \f$\theta\f$ | Log mass within \f$10^{-14}\f$ of the sum of the component log masses; derivative exact |
| Two-dimensional product, total mass | Zero-adjusted gamma1 times zero-adjusted gaussian2, with the four pieces (both coordinates, either one, neither) summed on grids of steps \f$2 \times 10^{-3}\f$ and \f$2 \times 10^{-2}\f$ | Within \f$10^{-8}\f$ of \f$1\f$ |
| Kernel and host | Values and finite-difference errors of the adapter inside a Kokkos kernel | Values within \f$10^{-13}\f$ of the host; errors below \f$10^{-7}\f$ |

## Example

The following host example, run after `Kokkos::initialize()`, constructs a lasso penalty on \f$4\f$ coefficients with a shared scale \f$\sigma = 0.6\f$ and a prior location \f$\mu_0\f$, and evaluates the penalty value and its gradient in \f$\beta\f$:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/distrib/independent_product.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/laplace1.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/univariate_as_multivariate.hpp>
#include <laplace/penalty/independent_product_penalty.hpp>

#include <vector>

double lasso_penalty_example(double (&grad)[4]) {
  using uam_laplace = laplace::distrib::univariate_as_multivariate<laplace::distrib::laplace1_distrib>;
  using lasso_dist = laplace::distrib::independent_product_distrib<uam_laplace>;

  const lasso_dist dist(laplace::distrib::independent_product_component<uam_laplace>{{}, 4, 1, nullptr});
  const double mu0[4] = {0.1, 0.0, -0.3, 0.2};
  const laplace::penalty::distrib_penalty<lasso_dist> pen(dist, mu0);

  std::vector<double> w(pen.state_size() + pen.scratch_size());
  const double theta[1] = {0.6};
  const double beta[4] = {0.7, -0.4, 0.5, 0.1};

  const auto st = pen.theta_state(theta, w.data());
  double* ws = w.data() + pen.state_size();
  const double val = pen.value(st, beta, ws);
  pen.value_dbeta_dk<1, 0>(st, beta, nullptr, grad, ws);
  return val;
}
\endcode

## Module types

- \ref laplace::distrib::univariate_as_multivariate_state "univariate_as_multivariate_state" — state of `univariate_as_multivariate<D>`
- \ref laplace::distrib::univariate_as_multivariate "univariate_as_multivariate\<D\>" — adapter from a continuous univariate distribution to dimension \f$1\f$
- \ref laplace::distrib::independent_product_component "independent_product_component\<M\>" — component distribution, copy count, and copy-to-set map
- \ref laplace::distrib::independent_product_state "independent_product_state" — state of `independent_product_distrib<M...>`
- \ref laplace::distrib::independent_product_distrib "independent_product_distrib\<M...\>" — product of independent multivariate blocks
- \ref laplace::penalty::univariate_penalty_reading "univariate_penalty_reading\<D\>" — scale-mixture and weak-convexity reading of a univariate law
- \ref laplace::penalty::distrib_penalty_traits "distrib_penalty_traits" — specializations for `univariate_as_multivariate<D>` and `independent_product_distrib<M...>`
*/
